  "PhotoPath": "",
  "Caption": "Our mobile app had retries. It made sense: if the network drops, try again.\n\nThen a customer wrote: \u0022I placed one order and got three.\u0022\n\nThe response had been lost on a weak network. The client saw a timeout and retried, but the server had already created the order. A timeout means \u0022I didn\u0027t get a response\u0022, not \u0022the server did nothing\u0022.\n\nThe fix: an Idempotency-Key. The client sends one unique key per intent and reuses it on every retry. The server stores the key with the response, and returns the stored response instead of creating a new order.\n\nDetails that matter:\n- Save the key and the order in one transaction\n- Put a unique index on the key\n- Same key with a different body returns 422\n- Give keys a TTL\n\nWhenever you add a retry, ask: what if the previous request actually succeeded?\n\nDo your APIs support idempotency keys?\n\n#API #Idempotency #DotNet #Backend",
